          <a href="#modvel-compression-sleeve"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/51dXy7ZGrAL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Modvel Compression Knee Sleeve (2-Pack)" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#mcdavid-hinged-knee-brace"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/3179wHz3ooL._SL500_.jpg" alt="McDavid Hinged Knee Brace" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Bauerfeind GenuTrain · Modvel Compression Knee Sleeve (2-Pack) · McDavid Hinged Knee Brace</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          The best knee brace for walking for most people is the Bauerfeind GenuTrain: a knitted
          compression sleeve with a built-in pad around the kneecap, comfortable enough to wear for a
          long walk and firm enough to make an aching knee feel steadier. If you want to spend less,
          a $20 two-pack of Modvel compression sleeves does the basic job. Walkers with a knee that
          gives way need a hinged brace such as McDavid&rsquo;s, and pain just below the kneecap is
          often better served by a strap. We have not tested these braces hands-on. Each pick was
          chosen from its Amazon listing, the maker&rsquo;s sizing and the published guidance on knee
          supports, and each card says which figures come from where.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
