        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best knee brace for walking?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most people, a compression sleeve with a kneecap pad, such as the Bauerfeind GenuTrain, at about $80 on Amazon in November 2026. If you want to spend less, a plain compression sleeve like Modvel's two-pack does the basic job for about $20. A knee that gives way needs a hinged brace instead.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I wear a knee brace when walking on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              If it makes walking more comfortable, yes. A brace is an aid that can help you walk more often, and walking more is what helps most knees. Put it on before you warm up, start on a flat belt, and take it off afterwards. See a doctor first if the knee is hot, swollen, locking or giving way.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a knee sleeve or a knee brace better for walking?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most walkers, a sleeve. Sleeves give warmth and gentle compression and are comfortable for long walks. Hinged braces are bulkier and warmer, and they are meant for knees that feel unstable. Straps suit pain in one spot just below the kneecap.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do knee braces help arthritis when walking?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They can help some people. The American College of Rheumatology's 2019 guideline strongly recommends knee braces for osteoarthritis when disability comes from instability, and conditionally recommends kneecap braces. England's NICE guideline advises against routine use without instability. Both put exercise first.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you wear a knee brace all day?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Most compression sleeves and activity braces are meant to be worn during activity rather than all day and night. Take it off after your walk, check the skin underneath, and do not sleep in it unless a clinician has told you to. If a brace leaves deep marks or causes numbness, it is too tight or the wrong size.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How tight should a knee brace be for walking?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Snug enough not to slide down after ten minutes of walking, but not so tight that it digs in behind the knee, leaves deep marks or causes tingling. Measure where the listing tells you to, with the knee slightly bent, and size up if you are near the top of a range.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

